\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Linear matter power spectrum $P_{\rm  m}(k)$ at $z=0$ for a cosmology without neutrinos, with massless neutrinos ($f_\nu = 0$), and with a neutrino fraction $f_\nu = \omega _\nu /\omega _m = 0.1$, computed with \textsc  {class} (\texttt  {vis/plot\_neutrino\_background.py}); cf.\ \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lesgourgues_massive_2006}. All three share $\omega _b$, $\omega _m$, and the primordial spectrum, so the offsets between them are due to the neutrino sector alone: the shifted turnover reflects the change in the epoch of equality (Figure~\ref {fig:transfer_function}), the small-scale suppression the free-streaming of the massive states.}}{8}{figure.1.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:neutrinos_effect}{{1.2}{8}{Linear matter power spectrum $P_{\rm m}(k)$ at $z=0$ for a cosmology without neutrinos, with massless neutrinos ($f_\nu = 0$), and with a neutrino fraction $f_\nu = \omega _\nu /\omega _m = 0.1$, computed with \textsc {class} (\texttt {vis/plot\_neutrino\_background.py}); cf.\ \cite {lesgourgues_massive_2006}. All three share $\omega _b$, $\omega _m$, and the primordial spectrum, so the offsets between them are due to the neutrino sector alone: the shifted turnover reflects the change in the epoch of equality (Figure~\ref {fig:transfer_function}), the small-scale suppression the free-streaming of the massive states}{figure.1.2}{}}
