\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Streaming shell accumulation. The integration grid is the sorted union of all shell edges, so each sub-step sweeps exactly one radial shell (highlighted). Per step, only the pre- and post-step particle states are alive; their lightcone crossings (Sec.~\ref {sec:crossing}) are pixelised and added to that shell's running count map, after which the pre-step state is discarded. Memory usage is thus two snapshots $+\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}S$ maps. $S$ is the number of shells in the \textsc  {CosmoGridV1} table (69), fixed by the data rather than by the integration: the step grid is built from the shell edges, so one sub-step sweeps one shell and no additional map is ever allocated for a finer time resolution.}}{19}{figure.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:shell_stream}{{2.3}{19}{Streaming shell accumulation. The integration grid is the sorted union of all shell edges, so each sub-step sweeps exactly one radial shell (highlighted). Per step, only the pre- and post-step particle states are alive; their lightcone crossings (Sec.~\ref {sec:crossing}) are pixelised and added to that shell's running count map, after which the pre-step state is discarded. Memory usage is thus two snapshots $+\;S$ maps. $S$ is the number of shells in the \textsc {CosmoGridV1} table (69), fixed by the data rather than by the integration: the step grid is built from the shell edges, so one sub-step sweeps one shell and no additional map is ever allocated for a finer time resolution}{figure.2.3}{}}
