\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Full-sky reconstruction from a patch model (\texttt  {ml/analysis/patch\_tiling.py}). Top: tiles are extracted by cached gnomonic index maps, corrected in batches, and blended with a radial cosine taper $w$ raised to a power $p$ before normalisation. Bottom: the weight profiles of three neighbouring tiles along a cut through the sky. At \mbox  {$p=1$} the profiles overlap broadly, so each pixel is an average over \mbox  {$N_{\mathrm  {eff}}$} tile predictions, harmless for the deterministic flow, whose overlapping predictions agree, but destructive for a stochastic sampler, whose independent samples average out. \new  {At \mbox  {$p\gg 1$} each profile collapses onto its tile centre, so every pixel is taken from essentially the single tile that sees it most centrally. The overlap is then no longer there to contribute values but to guarantee coverage: a convolutional network predicts an edge pixel poorly, because part of its receptive field falls outside the tile, so every sky pixel must lie in the \emph  {interior} of at least one tile. Overlapping the tiles ensures this, and \mbox  {$p=32$} then selects, for each pixel, the tile in whose interior it sits most deeply. The context is therefore the surrounding input pixels within that one tile, not information contributed by its neighbours, which is exactly the original overlap-tile argument \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ronneberger_u-net_2015}.} \textcolor {orange}{JB: what does this mean? What kind of context is provided by other tiles if p=32?}}}{24}{figure.2.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tiling}{{2.6}{24}{Full-sky reconstruction from a patch model (\texttt {ml/analysis/patch\_tiling.py}). Top: tiles are extracted by cached gnomonic index maps, corrected in batches, and blended with a radial cosine taper $w$ raised to a power $p$ before normalisation. Bottom: the weight profiles of three neighbouring tiles along a cut through the sky. At \mbox {$p=1$} the profiles overlap broadly, so each pixel is an average over \mbox {$N_{\mathrm {eff}}$} tile predictions, harmless for the deterministic flow, whose overlapping predictions agree, but destructive for a stochastic sampler, whose independent samples average out. \new {At \mbox {$p\gg 1$} each profile collapses onto its tile centre, so every pixel is taken from essentially the single tile that sees it most centrally. The overlap is then no longer there to contribute values but to guarantee coverage: a convolutional network predicts an edge pixel poorly, because part of its receptive field falls outside the tile, so every sky pixel must lie in the \emph {interior} of at least one tile. Overlapping the tiles ensures this, and \mbox {$p=32$} then selects, for each pixel, the tile in whose interior it sits most deeply. The context is therefore the surrounding input pixels within that one tile, not information contributed by its neighbours, which is exactly the original overlap-tile argument \cite {ronneberger_u-net_2015}.} \jozef {what does this mean? What kind of context is provided by other tiles if p=32?}}{figure.2.6}{}}
\newlabel{eq:transfer_def}{{2.18}{24}{Harmonic-Space Transfer-Function Pipeline}{equation.2.18}{}}
