\newlabel{eq:transfer_residual}{{2.19}{25}{Harmonic-Space Transfer-Function Pipeline}{equation.2.19}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces The harmonic apply chain (\texttt  {ml/transfer/apply\_transfer.py}). The correction is applied as a band-limited residual $(T-1)\,a_{\ell m}$ on top of the unmodified full-resolution shell $\delta _{\mathrm  {low}}$, as in Eq.~\eqref  {eq:transfer_residual}. The residual is confined to a band at both ends, and the two edges have different origins. Below \mbox  {$\ell _{\min }(s)$} the cutoff forces \mbox  {$T = 1$}, so \mbox  {$(T-1)\,a_{\ell m} = 0$} and \emph  {nothing is added} there. Since the residual is added to $\delta _{\mathrm  {low}}$ rather than replacing it, \mbox  {$a_{\ell m}^{\mathrm  {corr}} = a_{\ell m} + (T-1)\,a_{\ell m} = T\,a_{\ell m}$}, a vanishing residual leaves those modes exactly as \textsc  {Disco-DJ} produced them: this is what pins the already-correct large scales, and it is a hard step rather than a taper (Eq.~\eqref  {eq:ell_min}). Above \mbox  {$\ell _{\max }$} the residual is simply never computed, the transform having been truncated there. Reconstructing purely through \texttt  {alm2map} would band-limit the \emph  {output} to $\ell \le \ell _{\max }$ and discard genuine structure the $N_{\mathrm  {side}}{=}2048$ map holds beyond it (up to $\ell \sim 3N_{\mathrm  {side}}{-}1$) \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {sullivan_methods_2024}; adding the residual instead leaves both the large scales below \mbox  {$\ell _{\min }$} and the small scales above \mbox  {$\ell _{\max }$} exactly as $\delta _{\mathrm  {low}}$ had them.}}{25}{figure.2.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:transfer_flow}{{2.7}{25}{The harmonic apply chain (\texttt {ml/transfer/apply\_transfer.py}). The correction is applied as a band-limited residual $(T-1)\,a_{\ell m}$ on top of the unmodified full-resolution shell $\delta _{\mathrm {low}}$, as in Eq.~\eqref {eq:transfer_residual}. The residual is confined to a band at both ends, and the two edges have different origins. Below \mbox {$\ell _{\min }(s)$} the cutoff forces \mbox {$T = 1$}, so \mbox {$(T-1)\,a_{\ell m} = 0$} and \emph {nothing is added} there. Since the residual is added to $\delta _{\mathrm {low}}$ rather than replacing it, \mbox {$a_{\ell m}^{\mathrm {corr}} = a_{\ell m} + (T-1)\,a_{\ell m} = T\,a_{\ell m}$}, a vanishing residual leaves those modes exactly as \textsc {Disco-DJ} produced them: this is what pins the already-correct large scales, and it is a hard step rather than a taper (Eq.~\eqref {eq:ell_min}). Above \mbox {$\ell _{\max }$} the residual is simply never computed, the transform having been truncated there. Reconstructing purely through \texttt {alm2map} would band-limit the \emph {output} to $\ell \le \ell _{\max }$ and discard genuine structure the $N_{\mathrm {side}}{=}2048$ map holds beyond it (up to $\ell \sim 3N_{\mathrm {side}}{-}1$) \cite {sullivan_methods_2024}; adding the residual instead leaves both the large scales below \mbox {$\ell _{\min }$} and the small scales above \mbox {$\ell _{\max }$} exactly as $\delta _{\mathrm {low}}$ had them}{figure.2.7}{}}
