\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces The emulated transfer function $T(\ell , s)$ (left) and the phase correlation $r(\ell , s)$ (right), for six shells spanning the lightcone of the held-out cosmology \texttt  {cosmo\_000176}, coloured by shell redshift (\texttt  {vis/plot\_transfer\_function.py}, run \texttt  {2986895}, the same $\ell _{\max }=1500$ emulator that produces the results of Sec.~\ref {sec:res-transfer}). $T$ is shown as the emulator predicts it, before the per-shell cutoff of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:ell_min} forces \mbox  {$T = 1$} below \mbox  {$\ell _{\min }(s)$} at correction time. Thin grey lines are the same two quantities predicted for the other 29 held-out cosmologies at the same six shells: on the left they fan out visibly, showing that $T$ is genuinely cosmology-dependent and largest where the correction itself is largest; on the right they are invisible, coincident with the coloured curves, because $r(\ell ,s)$ is not emulated per cosmology at all -- it is a single training-set average copied into every held-out prediction. The amplitude deficit to be repaired grows towards low redshift and towards small scales, while the phases stay correlated ($r \to 1$) precisely on the shells and scales where the amplitude rescaling is the appropriate correction.}}{26}{figure.2.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:transfer_function_learned}{{2.8}{26}{The emulated transfer function $T(\ell , s)$ (left) and the phase correlation $r(\ell , s)$ (right), for six shells spanning the lightcone of the held-out cosmology \texttt {cosmo\_000176}, coloured by shell redshift (\texttt {vis/plot\_transfer\_function.py}, run \texttt {2986895}, the same $\ell _{\max }=1500$ emulator that produces the results of Sec.~\ref {sec:res-transfer}). $T$ is shown as the emulator predicts it, before the per-shell cutoff of Eq.~\eqref {eq:ell_min} forces \mbox {$T = 1$} below \mbox {$\ell _{\min }(s)$} at correction time. Thin grey lines are the same two quantities predicted for the other 29 held-out cosmologies at the same six shells: on the left they fan out visibly, showing that $T$ is genuinely cosmology-dependent and largest where the correction itself is largest; on the right they are invisible, coincident with the coloured curves, because $r(\ell ,s)$ is not emulated per cosmology at all -- it is a single training-set average copied into every held-out prediction. The amplitude deficit to be repaired grows towards low redshift and towards small scales, while the phases stay correlated ($r \to 1$) precisely on the shells and scales where the amplitude rescaling is the appropriate correction}{figure.2.8}{}}
