\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces Scalar conditioning in the shared U-Net (\texttt  {FlowUNet} / \texttt  {DenoiserUNet}). (a) The scalar, flow time \mbox  {$t$} or noise level \mbox  {$c_{\mathrm  {noise}}(\sigma )$}, is mapped to 64 geometrically spaced sinusoids (with their cosines) and expanded by a two-layer MLP to a 512-dimensional embedding, encoding position along the trajectory from coarse (low frequencies) to fine (high). \new  {(b) A block holds \mbox  {$C$} feature maps \mbox  {$h_c \in \mathbb  {R}^{H \times W}$}, and each of them is to be rescaled and offset. That needs two numbers per channel, \mbox  {$\gamma _c$} and \mbox  {$\beta _c$}, hence \mbox  {$2C$} numbers in total, which is what the block's own linear layer produces from the shared 512-dimensional embedding; the output is simply split in half, the first \mbox  {$C$} values giving the scales and the second \mbox  {$C$} the shifts.} They are applied between the two convolutions as $h_c(i,j)\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow (1+\gamma _c)\,h_c(i,j)+\beta _c$, the same pair at every pixel $(i,j)$, so the modulation changes how strongly each channel speaks rather than what it looks at. \new  {(c) The same embedding modulates every encoder, bottleneck and decoder block; each box gives the number of channels above and the spatial size below, so that resolution falls by a factor of two per level while the channel count doubles.} The cosmology--redshift vector enters once, additively and zero-initialised, at the bottleneck.}}{28}{figure.2.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:film_unet}{{2.9}{28}{Scalar conditioning in the shared U-Net (\texttt {FlowUNet} / \texttt {DenoiserUNet}). (a) The scalar, flow time \mbox {$t$} or noise level \mbox {$c_{\mathrm {noise}}(\sigma )$}, is mapped to 64 geometrically spaced sinusoids (with their cosines) and expanded by a two-layer MLP to a 512-dimensional embedding, encoding position along the trajectory from coarse (low frequencies) to fine (high). \new {(b) A block holds \mbox {$C$} feature maps \mbox {$h_c \in \mathbb {R}^{H \times W}$}, and each of them is to be rescaled and offset. That needs two numbers per channel, \mbox {$\gamma _c$} and \mbox {$\beta _c$}, hence \mbox {$2C$} numbers in total, which is what the block's own linear layer produces from the shared 512-dimensional embedding; the output is simply split in half, the first \mbox {$C$} values giving the scales and the second \mbox {$C$} the shifts.} They are applied between the two convolutions as $h_c(i,j)\mapsto (1+\gamma _c)\,h_c(i,j)+\beta _c$, the same pair at every pixel $(i,j)$, so the modulation changes how strongly each channel speaks rather than what it looks at. \new {(c) The same embedding modulates every encoder, bottleneck and decoder block; each box gives the number of channels above and the spatial size below, so that resolution falls by a factor of two per level while the channel count doubles.} The cosmology--redshift vector enters once, additively and zero-initialised, at the bottleneck}{figure.2.9}{}}
