\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces All three corrections on one axis (\texttt  {vis/plot\_method\_comparison.py}), for DES-Y3 \textsc  {metacal} source bin~1 (top) and bin~4 (bottom). For each of the 30 held-out cosmologies the $\kappa $-map variance, skewness and excess kurtosis are divided by their \textsc  {CosmoGridV1} reference value; the marker is the mean of that ratio over cosmologies and the errorbar its standard deviation, so a perfect correction sits on the dashed line at unity with a short bar. The errorbar measures cosmology-to-cosmology \emph  {consistency}, not uncertainty on the mean: a method may sit on unity with a wide bar (right on average, unreliable per cosmology) or slightly below it with a tight bar (biased but predictable). The uncorrected \textsc  {Disco-DJ} lightcone is shown on the same axis. The distance from it to unity is the gap each method is trying to close. All three are evaluated at the common $N_{\mathrm  {side}}=512$, $\ell _{\max }=1500$ footing, which handicaps the transfer function relative to its native-resolution capability (see the common-footing discussion in Sec.~\ref {sec:res-protocol}).}}{49}{figure.3.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method_comparison}{{3.19}{49}{All three corrections on one axis (\texttt {vis/plot\_method\_comparison.py}), for DES-Y3 \textsc {metacal} source bin~1 (top) and bin~4 (bottom). For each of the 30 held-out cosmologies the $\kappa $-map variance, skewness and excess kurtosis are divided by their \textsc {CosmoGridV1} reference value; the marker is the mean of that ratio over cosmologies and the errorbar its standard deviation, so a perfect correction sits on the dashed line at unity with a short bar. The errorbar measures cosmology-to-cosmology \emph {consistency}, not uncertainty on the mean: a method may sit on unity with a wide bar (right on average, unreliable per cosmology) or slightly below it with a tight bar (biased but predictable). The uncorrected \textsc {Disco-DJ} lightcone is shown on the same axis. The distance from it to unity is the gap each method is trying to close. All three are evaluated at the common $N_{\mathrm {side}}=512$, $\ell _{\max }=1500$ footing, which handicaps the transfer function relative to its native-resolution capability (see the common-footing discussion in Sec.~\ref {sec:res-protocol})}{figure.3.19}{}}
